    "question": {
      "math_academy_id": "q-221837",
      "sequence_position": 9,
      "problem": "Disprove the following statement:\n\n$-4x-{x}^{2}\\le 0$ for any $x\\in R.$\n\nTo {{field-1}} a universal statement, it's sufficient to find a {{field-2}}. \n\nIn other words, we need to show that {{field-3}}\n\n$$\n-4x-{x}^{2}{{field-4}}0.\n$$\n\nBy substituting $x=$ {{field-5}} into the expression, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-4x-{x}^{2} & =-4({{field-6}})-{({{field-7}})}^{2} \\\\  & ={{field-8}} \\\\  & {{field-9}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, $x=$ {{field-10}} is our counterexample. Therefore, the given statement is false.",
      "worked_solution": "Our statement can be written as\n\n$$\n∀x\\in R,\\,-4x-{x}^{2}\\le 0.\n$$\n\nWe want to disprove this statement. In other words, we must show that its negation is true:\n\n$$\n¬(∀x\\in R,\\,-4x-{x}^{2}\\le 0)\\,≡\\,∃x\\in R,\\,-4x-{x}^{2}>0\n$$\n\nSo, we begin as follows:\n\nTo disprove a universal statement, it's sufficient to find a counterexample. In other words, we need to show that there exists a real number $x$ such that \n\n$$\n-4x-{x}^{2}>0.\n$$\n\nFinding a counterexample is sometimes a nontrivial task, but we only need one. In this case, substituting $x=-2$ suffices:\n\nBy substituting $x=-2$ into the expression, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-4x-{x}^{2} & =-4(-2)-(-2)^{2} \\\\  & =4 \\\\  & >0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we state our conclusion:\n\nSo, $x=-2$ is our counterexample. Therefore, the given statement is false.",
